Why Is Demand Increasing?
Water conservation requirements: Growing pressure to reduce water waste is encouraging farmers, municipalities, and property owners to adopt automated irrigation.
Precision agriculture adoption: Sensor-based scheduling helps align water delivery with crop needs and changing weather conditions.
Connected irrigation technology: IoT-enabled controllers allow users to monitor systems remotely and adjust watering schedules using digital platforms.

Smart Agriculture Watch: Data-Driven Irrigation
Irrigation controllers are increasingly integrating weather forecasts, soil-moisture data, mobile applications, and cloud-based analytics. These features support more responsive watering schedules and help users monitor irrigation performance. High initial costs, connectivity limitations in rural areas, and compatibility with older irrigation infrastructure can slow adoption, particularly among smaller farms.

